ithout us having looked at  the page you know when looking at the  page no and making any estimates and  this is just one one diagram showing  giving sort of the gist of the technique  which is to look at the page look at  sections of the page look at subsections  of the page and what we do every time we  subdivide the page is we compute  correlation coefficients how how how  much is one one point and the page like  another point and these horizontal lines  indicate you know directions where  things are staying the same whereas when  they when they go from from white to  black things are varying so we can see  where there's a picture we don't see any  nice neat patterns but when we have txt  we see this very regularly periodic  pattern and we can compute from that  from the size of the periods what the  text height is now obviously not you  know we since it's not all text that's  why we do this break it down break it  down so when we have mainly picture we  get no period we get something DISA T  that's a little bit interrupted when  there's a bit of illustration in with  the text and we get a nice clean signal  when we have all text so we go through  all the pages and perform this analysis  and get an estimate of text type for  each piece at each level and then by  combining those estimates for each page  ultimately getting an estimate for each  page for the text that's on that page  what is the typical height so it's more  you know blatantly obvious in this  text in this printed edition that we get  36 pixels indicated by that little red  box so we we took this algorithm and ran  it on about 7,000 manuscript pages and  here are some typical results of various  kinds of pages that have different  combinations of text and an image and  one question you say well great you  ranted on 7,000 pages how do you know  yet the right answer that that's a  difficult question also first of all we  took a hundred pages at random and went  through and actually measured how high  t