 the rods in  your eye work and so just to to  summarize then we can get get  comparisons of what the materials look  like at different points in the page is  the flourishing does that seem to be the  same pigment as say the rubric was on  the page we've since that example we've  improved our equipment we use eight  bands now and the people actually doing  this aren't here because they are on our  tour of the UK they've been in Oxford  Cambridge this week and then they're  going back to Wales and their imaging a  number of manuscripts for support of  this project and the next step in that  is there going to be doing things like a  doing massive studies of multiple pages  to analyze the changes in in pigment  spectra and the the variations of  pigment spectra on on various pages and  also we're developing a tool for  scholars to use so they can go in and  probe and look at am I seeing really a  difference in color are these are these  really different and a way of exporting  their observations so that's just a  couple of works in progress and the  people actually doing the work rogerio  Pentos and yang Yang as I said they  should be here presenting it but they're  they're traveling and with the support  from the larger project from YDC to Meg  ballinger Lewis King Michael Appleby and  then Jessica Brantley and Barbara  schaller the scholars actually  interested in the manuscripts that were  we're trying to analyze and this is all  of melon funded project thank you thank  you very much

